writing.prompt:
You are Lic. Arturo Peña, the insurance adjuster who attended the collision in lesson 02 (an invented event; all names are invented too). The car belongs to **Carmen Ruiz Ochoa**, 72, the policyholder. She wasn't there, she doesn't know insurance vocabulary, and she has asked you to explain in writing what happened and what she has to do. Her daughter Mariana was driving.

This is your own claim report (*reporte de siniestro*), version 1 of the lesson:

> REPORTE DE SINIESTRO 2026-03-0417. 03/03/2026, 08:40 h. Av. Insurgentes Sur, col. Del Valle, CDMX. Asegurada: Carmen Ruiz Ochoa, póliza AU-55821, cobertura amplia. Conductora: Mariana Solís Ruiz, ilesa. Vehículo tercero (camioneta de reparto, placas no identificadas) realiza cambio de carril sin precaución y colisiona con salpicadera delantera derecha y cofre; tercero se da a la fuga. Daños: abolladuras en salpicadera y cofre; carrocería sin daño estructural. Lesionados: un motociclista, leve. Se elabora croquis. Procede peritaje. Aplica deducible.

Two more facts you may use: the policy's deductible is **5% of the car's commercial value**, and the appraisal (*peritaje*) will be done at the insurer's workshop on **Thursday, March 5**, where the repair cost will be set. Add no other facts.

Write a **letter or e-mail to Mrs. Ruiz** (200–250 words, the length of the DELE C2's shorter writing tasks) that:

- addresses her as *usted*, respectfully and warmly, from the first line to the signature;
- tells her what happened, keeping every fact of the report (date, time, place, who was hurt and how badly, what was damaged, that the van's driver fled) and adding none;
- explains in plain Spanish what *siniestro*, *cobertura amplia*, *peritaje* and *deducible* mean for her, without copying the report's jargon or abbreviations;
- tells her why she pays the deductible in this case and what happens next;
- closes with an offer to answer her questions and a courteous sign-off.

Mariana's statement in the lesson shows the opposite move (from colloquial to formal). Here you go from specialized to clear and warm, without becoming colloquial.
